    "result_text": "The sheet now has a \"period numbering from\" setting, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The setting:** it's a date, or blank for off. It's added to the settings form and settings list, and the server accepts only a blank or a valid date. Changing it writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=period-numbering-from value=<date>` (the value is empty when cleared), like the other settings.\n- **Numbering:** when a claim is opened and the setting is in force (the sheet's today is on or after the date), the claim gets `<period name>-<n>`, counting from 1 in each period. A claim in no period gets `X-<n>` from its own counter. Otherwise it gets the next sheet-wide number. Period numbering doesn't use up sheet-wide numbers, so after the setting is cleared the sheet-wide numbers carry on from where they stopped. In the first test, that's `1`, `2`, then `X-1`, `P1-1`, `P2-1`, then `3`. This is in the new `ClaimNumbering` service, which both single open and bulk open use.\n- **Claim numbers are now text:** a new migration (`V21__period_numbering.sql`) turns the number column, and the column holding the number a correction refers to, from whole numbers into text. Existing numbers keep their values. The migration also adds a table holding one counter per period, plus one for X.\n- **Corrections:** a correction can now name a period number such as `X-1`, which the test needs. Plain numbers are checked as before (\"0\" is still refused, \"01\" still means 1).\n\n**Behaviours to know about**\n- Period counters are keyed by the period's name, ignoring case. Nothing in the app renames a period today. If renaming is ever added, a renamed period would start counting from 1 again.\n- A number takes the name the period had when the claim was opened.\n- Period numbering checks every period whose dates contain the claim's date, including closed ones. In practice opening is already refused for claims in a closed period, so this doesn't come up.",
